            <ArticleTitle>Infection with Echinostoma sp. in a group of travellers to Lake Tanganyika, Tanzania, in January 2017.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/jtm/tax036</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A small group of travellers became infected with Echinostoma sp. after ingesting raw fish which they caught in Lake Tanganyika, Tanzania, in January 2017. The infection was diagnosed by finding the characteristic eggs in stool samples collected over a 2-week period following their return to Kenya. Echinostoma is a genus of parasitic flukes normally known to infect humans in southeast Asia and the Far East. This appears to be the first report of echinostomiasis in East Africa which can be attributed clearly to ingestion of locally caught raw fish.</AbstractText>
